# Consumer Behaviour and Utility: Why We Buy What We Buy

> Every purchase is a small vote about what makes us better off. This
> lesson builds the theory of the consumer step by step: from utility
> and its measurement to indifference curves, budget lines, and surplus.

## 1. What Is Utility?

[definition]
**Utility** is the want-satisfying power of a good or service — the
satisfaction, however subjective, that a consumer expects from
consuming it. It is *expected* satisfaction, not usefulness: a packet
of cigarettes has utility to a smoker even though it harms health.
[/definition]

Utility is personal and depends on time, place, and circumstance. A
cup of cutting chai at a Mumbai railway platform in the monsoon has
far more utility than the same cup on a hot afternoon. Economics
treats utility as *subjective* — the same good can carry different
utility to two people, which is why prices alone cannot measure it.

## 2. Two Approaches: Cardinal and Ordinal

Economists split into two camps over whether satisfaction can be
measured like weight or temperature.

@diagram tree id="utility-approaches" layout="tree" aria="Cardinal and ordinal approaches to consumer theory"
  "Theory of Consumer Behaviour" -> "Cardinal approach (Marshall)"
  "Theory of Consumer Behaviour" -> "Ordinal approach (Hicks and Allen)"
  "Cardinal approach (Marshall)" -> "Total and marginal utility in utils"
  "Cardinal approach (Marshall)" -> "Law of equi-marginal utility"
  "Ordinal approach (Hicks and Allen)" -> "Indifference curves"
  "Ordinal approach (Hicks and Allen)" -> "Budget line and optimum choice"
@/diagram

| Feature | Cardinal approach | Ordinal approach |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Can utility be measured? | Yes, in imaginary units ("utils") | No, only ranked: better, worse, equal |
| Key economist | Alfred Marshall (1890) | J. R. Hicks and R. G. D. Allen (1934) |
| Main tools | TU, MU curves | Indifference curves and budget line |
| Equilibrium condition | MU of last rupee equal in all uses | MRS equals price ratio |

The ordinal approach won the profession because satisfaction is
obviously not measurable in numbers — you cannot say tea gives
exactly *twice* the utility of coffee. But ranking choices is easy
and enough to predict behaviour.

## 3. Total Utility and Marginal Utility

[definition]
**Total utility (TU)** is the satisfaction from consuming all units of
a good taken together. **Marginal utility (MU)** is the *addition* to
total utility from consuming one more unit: MU = change in TU
divided by change in quantity.
[/definition]

Watch the two move together as a student drinks successive cups of
tea on a winter evening:

| Cup | Marginal utility (utils) | Total utility (utils) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | 20 | 20 |
| 2 | 14 | 34 |
| 3 | 8 | 42 |
| 4 | 2 | 44 |
| 5 | 0 | 44 |
| 6 | -4 | 40 |

@chart line title="Marginal and total utility of successive cups of tea (utils)" labels:
1
2
3
4
5
6

series:
  - name: "Marginal utility"
    data: 20 14 8 2 0 -4
  - name: "Total utility"
    data: 20 34 42 44 44 40
@/chart

Three relationships follow directly:

- While **MU is positive**, TU keeps rising (slower and slower).
- When **MU = 0**, TU is at its maximum — the **point of satiety**.
- When **MU turns negative**, TU *falls* — the sixth cup is a regret.

## 4. The Law of Diminishing Marginal Utility

[definition]
As a consumer takes **successive equal units of the same commodity at
the same time**, the marginal utility of each additional unit keeps
falling. The first plate of biryani delights; the fifth, after a long
day, barely tempts.
[/definition]

The law rests on assumptions worth listing, because exam questions
probe them: units must be **homogeneous** (same size and quality),
consumption must be **continuous**, units must be of **reasonable
size** (sips of water, not a teaspoon at a time), the consumer's
**tastes must stay constant**, and the consumer must be **rational**.

[warning]
The law is a tendency, not a universal rule. It fails for hobbies and
collections (a stamp collector values the fiftieth rare stamp more
than the second), for addictive consumption, and for very small
initial doses of intensely wanted goods. For most ordinary goods over
a normal consumption span, however, diminishing MU is reliable — and
it is the bedrock of the downward-sloping demand curve.
[/warning]

[tip]
Memory hook: *diminishing MU explains "variety is the spice of
life"* — and it explains why Indian FMCG firms sell ₹1 and ₹5 shampoo
sachets. A small, cheap unit fits the shrinking marginal utility of a
low-income consumer's budget.
[/tip]

## 5. Consumer Equilibrium: The Law of Equi-Marginal Utility

A consumer with a fixed money income faces many goods. Marshall's
rule: allocate spending so that the **marginal utility per rupee is
equal in every use**.

[important]
Equilibrium requires **MUx / Px = MUy / Py** (extended to all goods).
If the ratio is higher for X than Y, shifting one rupee from Y to X
raises total utility — so the consumer keeps shifting until the
ratios are equal.
[/important]

Worked example. Income ₹30, price of good X is ₹2, price of good Y is
₹3. Suppose MU schedules are such that at 6 units of X and 6 units of
Y the per-rupee utilities are 6 and 6. Check affordability: 6 × 2 + 6
× 3 = ₹30 — the budget is exactly spent and no rearrangement can
raise total utility. Now suppose prices of X fall to ₹1. MUx/Px
rises above MUy/Py, so the consumer buys more X and less Y — which is
precisely why the demand curve slopes downward.

## 6. Indifference Curves and the Marginal Rate of Substitution

The ordinal approach represents preferences with **indifference
curves** — each curve shows combinations of two goods giving the
consumer *equal* satisfaction.

@diagram tree id="ic-properties" layout="tree" aria="Properties of indifference curves"
  "Indifference curve properties" -> "Downward sloping to the right"
  "Indifference curve properties" -> "Convex to the origin"
  "Indifference curve properties" -> "Higher curve shows higher satisfaction"
  "Indifference curve properties" -> "Two curves never intersect"
@/diagram

The **slope** of an indifference curve at any point is the **marginal
rate of substitution (MRS)** — the amount of good Y the consumer is
willing to give up for one more unit of good X with utility
unchanged. Convexity means MRS *diminishes*: the more X you already
hold, the less Y you will sacrifice for yet another unit of X. If
MRS stayed constant the curve would be a straight line; perfect
substitutes and perfect complements are the two special cases that
exam questions love.

[warning]
A common error: believing two indifference curves can cross. They
cannot. At a crossing point the common bundle would have to yield two
different levels of satisfaction at once — a logical contradiction.
[/warning]

## 7. The Budget Line and the Consumer's Optimum

[definition]
The **budget line** (price line) shows all combinations of two goods
that exactly exhaust the consumer's income at given prices:
**Px·X + Py·Y = M**. Its slope equals **Px / Py** — the market's rate
of exchange between the goods.
[/definition]

Two movements matter:

- **Income change**: a rise in income shifts the budget line
  *parallel* outward; a fall shifts it inward. Slope is unchanged
  because prices have not moved.
- **Price change**: a fall in Px *pivots* the line outward along the
  X-axis, flattening the slope; the Y-intercept stays put.

Consumer equilibrium is where an indifference curve is *tangent* to
the budget line: **MRS = Px/Py**. At that point the consumer's
willingness to trade (MRS) exactly matches the market's terms of
trade (price ratio). Anywhere else, a shift of spending towards the
good with the higher "bang per rupee" increases satisfaction.

[example]
Suppose a college student has ₹120 for snacks, samosas cost ₹20 and
cold coffee ₹60. The slope is 20/60 = 1/3: three samosas trade for
one cold coffee. If a campus discount cuts cold coffee to ₹40, the
line pivots outward on the coffee axis and the student typically
buys more coffee — a price effect composed of a substitution effect
(relative price cheaper) and an income effect (real purchasing power
higher).
[/example]

## 8. Consumer Surplus and Real-World Insights

[definition]
**Consumer surplus** is the difference between what a consumer is
*willing to pay* for a good and what is *actually paid*. Marshall:
the surplus of satisfaction a consumer enjoys because the market
price is below the maximum willingness to pay.
[/definition]

[example]
A cricket fan would pay up to ₹2,000 for an India match ticket but
the printed price is ₹800. The consumer surplus is ₹1,200. Consumers
of essentials such as salt enjoy enormous surplus relative to a
price of a few rupees — the good's total utility is huge while its
marginal utility, and hence its price, is tiny.
[/example]

That last observation resolves the classic **water–diamond paradox**
of Adam Smith's era: water has immense total utility but low marginal
utility (it is abundant), so its price is low; diamonds are scarce,
so their marginal utility — and price — is high. Price tracks
*marginal* value, not total importance.

## 9. Quick Revision

| Concept | One-line summary |
| --- | --- |
| Utility | Want-satisfying power; subjective |
| Cardinal vs ordinal | Measurable in utils vs only rankable |
| MU = 0 | TU maximum — point of satiety |
| Law of DMU | Successive units give shrinking MU |
| Equi-marginal rule | MUx/Px = MUy/Py at equilibrium |
| Indifference curve | Equal-satisfaction combinations; convex, non-intersecting |
| MRS | Slope of IC; diminishes along the curve |
| Budget line | Px·X + Py·Y = M; slope Px/Py |
| Tangency condition | MRS = Px/Py |
| Consumer surplus | Willingness to pay minus actual price |
